The standard approach in .NET is to **abstract file I/O behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into your classes instead of calling `System.IO` directly. In tests, you mock/stub the interface.

**Alternatives:**

1. **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — a popular NuGet package that wraps the entire `System.IO` namespace behind interfaces (`IFileSystem`, `IFile`, `IDirectory`) with an in-memory test filesystem included.

2. **Thin wrapper + `virtual` methods** — if DI feels heavy for a small project, wrap calls in a class with `virtual` methods and override in tests.

**Recommendation:** Use [`System.IO.Abstractions`](https://www.nuget.org/packages/System.IO.Abstractions) — it's well-maintained, saves boilerplate, and its `MockFileSystem` makes tests straightforward:

```csharp
// Production
services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();

// Test
var fs = new MockFileSystem();
fs.AddFile("/data.txt", new MockFileData("hello"));
var sut = new MyService(fs);
```
